To connect a bumper switch that stops the robot after touching an object, follow these steps based on the provided course materials:

1. **Connections**:
   - Connect the push-button switch (PBSW) terminals as follows:
     | PBSW con. | RobDuino connectors |
     |:---------:|:-------------------:|
     |   No. 1   |          A0         |
     |   No. 2   |         GND         |
     |   No. 3   |         +5V         |

   This setup is specified in the course materials under the section discussing the construction of the bumper for detection【4:0†source】【4:1†source】.

2. **Testing the Setup**: 
   - After connecting, you can test it with a simple C++ program. Below is a sample code that you can use:
   ```cpp
   const int BUMPER_PIN = A0; // Assuming A0 is connected to the bumper
   const int TEST_BUMPER_LED_PIN = 3; // An LED connected to pin 3
   void setup() {
     pinMode(BUMPER_PIN, INPUT);
     pinMode(TEST_BUMPER_LED_PIN, OUTPUT);
   }

   void loop() {
     bool bumperIsPressed = digitalRead(BUMPER_PIN);
     if (bumperIsPressed) {
       digitalWrite(TEST_BUMPER_LED_PIN, HIGH); // Turn on LED when bumper is pressed
     } else {
       digitalWrite(TEST_BUMPER_LED_PIN, LOW); // Turn off LED when bumper is not pressed
     }
   }
   ```

3. **Concrete Experiment**:
   - Implement this program and test the bumper switch by physically pressing it. When the bumper is pressed, the LED should light up, indicating that the switch is activated. This will confirm that the bumper switch is wired correctly and functioning as intended【4:0†source】【4:1†source】.

